Make LÖVE build for visionOS, and let it render without a presentable layer.

Part 1 -- building at all. visionOS defines TARGET_OS_IPHONE, so config.h
already takes the LOVE_IOS branch and the whole iOS backend compiles, which is
what we want: Metal, the iOS filesystem layout, the SDL3 UIKit path. Three
things stop it:

  1. src/common/ios.mm uses UIScreen in showGameList. visionOS has no UIScreen
     because there is no single "main screen" to take bounds from.
     showGameList is LÖVE's picker for choosing among several .love files and
     is unreachable in an app that fuses one game.love into its bundle, so it
     only has to compile.

  2. src/modules/graphics/Graphics.cpp calls opengl::createInstance()
     unconditionally, while the Vulkan and Metal backends beside it are already
     guarded. Every GLES symbol in XROS.sdk is API_UNAVAILABLE(visionos), so
     there is no OpenGL backend to link.

  3. Hence a matching LOVE_GRAPHICS_OPENGL feature macro in config.h, defined
     everywhere except visionOS, so renderer selection can be guarded the same
     way as Vulkan and Metal already are.

(2) and (3) are worth upstreaming on their own: they make "a platform with no
OpenGL backend" expressible, which is a gap in LÖVE today rather than anything
visionOS-specific.

Part 2 -- rendering with nowhere to present. A fully immersive visionOS app
has no CAMetalLayer: frames go to CompositorServices drawables, and the flat
2D image becomes a texture the app shows in a window or in-world. So
metal::Graphics::setMode now accepts a null context and stands an ordinary
offscreen texture in for the drawable. Everything downstream -- render passes,
MSAA resolve, screenshot readback -- keeps working against that texture
unchanged; only presentDrawable is skipped, because there is nothing to
present to.

Whether to do that is a RUNTIME choice, not a compile-time one, and that
distinction matters: the flat visionOS build still presents through SDL's
layer exactly as on iOS and must keep working, while the immersive shell asks
for a headless backbuffer before booting LÖVE. Window.cpp consults
love_visionos_getHeadless() rather than an #ifdef for exactly that reason.

SDL's window is left alone in both cases rather than suppressed. It is never
presented in the headless case, but it remains the source of events, and
leaving it keeps this patch to one condition instead of auditing every Window
method for a null window handle.

Verified: the flat build still renders its launcher in the simulator after
these changes.

